#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231810} :activity/id #uuid "60204b9d-c0a9-5524-bd30-c8accd8e6729" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049479 :topic/id #uuid "f868b857-f4b2-5b40-8da2-046b9db36a57" :topic/math-academy-id 6721} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231810 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231984 :tutorial/content "We know how to find a Fourier-series particular solution $y_p$ for a periodically forced linear ODE.\nIn this topic, the new step is how to use $y_p$ to satisfy an initial condition.\n\nRecall that once a particular solution $y_p$ is chosen, the full solution has the form\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p,\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c$ solves the corresponding homogeneous equation.\n\nNow, suppose the IVP is given at $x=x_0{:}$\n\n$$\ny(x_0)=y_0.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\ny_0 = y_c(x_0)+y_p(x_0).\n$$\n\nA useful simplification is to choose the particular solution so that\n\n$$\ny_p(x_0)=0.\n$$\n\nIn that case, the initial condition becomes\n\n$$\ny_0 = y_c(x_0),\n$$\n\nso we can solve for the constant(s) in $y_c$ immediately (without evaluating any Fourier sums at $x_0$).\n\nOn the next slide, we’ll apply this idea in an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "4a6bfce3-0ee4-5ea7-905f-461e3f15500e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14713 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1d2d573d-acda-5605-a740-470ab9c3110a" :step/math-academy-id 607581 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231811}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231811 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231993 :tutorial/content "For example, consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'-2y = f(x), \\qquad y(0)=3,\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Suppose we choose the forcing (and hence the Fourier-series particular solution) so that\n\n$$\ny_p(0)=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation:\n\n$$\ny' - 2y = 0\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny_c = Ae^{2x}.\n$$\n\nA $2$-periodic forcing function can be written using harmonics of frequency $\\pi$, so we may write the particular solution in the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(a_n\\cos(n\\pi x)+b_n\\sin(n\\pi x)\\right).\n$$\n\nThus the full solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=Ae^{2x}+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(a_n\\cos(n\\pi x)+b_n\\sin(n\\pi x)\\right).\n$$\n\nNow apply $y(0)=3$ and use $y_p(0)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A{e}^{2(0)}+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 3 & =A(1)+0 \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ny(x)=\n3e^{2x}+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(a_n\\cos(n\\pi x)+b_n\\sin(n\\pi x)\\right).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ae6c43bc-e554-5975-9433-c7d67c2adbdd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14804 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "48c86314-12fc-57dd-bb24-3dab5839b9c1" :step/math-academy-id 607582 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231812}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231812 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231418 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231291 :question/id #uuid "b7a81b9e-d12c-55c7-b755-8f4e5b8115c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-21859"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "742531ae-be2d-5fc8-8ef8-4f745c93bacd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049288}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508036}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order IVPs Using Fourier Series"} :step/id #uuid "26e21ed5-3121-54a7-8a6b-1264c940acaa" :step/math-academy-id 607583 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231813}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231813 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231989 :tutorial/content "When we solve a second-order IVP using Fourier series, we still represent the solution in the form\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p,\n$$\n\nbut since the homogeneous solution has *two* constants, we use *two* conditions:\n\n$$\ny(0)=y_0\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny'(0)=y_1\n$$\n\nBefore we start, we can use the forcing series properties to simplify our work. For a second-order equation of the form \n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = f(x)\n$$\n\nwith no $y'$ (damping) term, the particular solution can be simplified. Here, we have two cases:\n\n- **Case 1:** $f(x)$ is odd.\n\nIf $f(x)$ is *odd* and the equation has the form $y''+\\omega^2 y=f(x)$, then the particular solution is also *odd*, so it is a sine series:\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right)\n$$\n\nThis immediately gives $y_p(0)=0,$ so the condition $y(0)=y_0$ determines the cosine constant in $y_c$ without evaluating any infinite sum at $0$.\n\n- **Case 2:** $f(x)$ is even.\n\nIf $f(x)$ is *even* and the equation has the form $y''+\\omega^2 y=f(x)$, then the particular solution is also *even*, so it is a cosine series:\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right)\n$$\n\nHere, the derivative is a sine series:\n\n$$\ny_p'(x)=-\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{L} \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right)\n$$\n\nThis immediately gives $y_p'(0)=0,$ so the condition $y'(0)=y_1$ determines the sine constant in $y_c$ without evaluating any infinite sum at $0$.\n\nIn both cases, the key idea is that evaluating at $x=0$ (and differentiating once) lets the initial conditions pick off the constants in $y_c$ as cleanly as possible.\n\nLet's see how this works in practice on the next slide." :tutorial/id #uuid "c8c0ab68-168a-57f1-9ab6-13bd9d042d32" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14781 :tutorial/title "Second-Order IVPs"} :step/id #uuid "ffe83dea-ed44-5451-a483-e07ad7977f77" :step/math-academy-id 607586 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231814}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231814 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231990 :tutorial/content "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny''+ 7y = f(x), \\qquad y(0)=3,\\quad y'(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is an even, $2\\pi$-periodic function. Since the forcing is even and the differential operator contains no $y'$ term, we represent the particular solution as a cosine series:\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n\\cos(nx)\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the homogeneous equation $y''+7y=0.$ The characteristic equation is $\\lambda^2+7=0,$ so\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos(\\sqrt{7}x)+B\\sin(\\sqrt{7}x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Differentiating the total solution gives\n\n$$\ny'(x)=-A\\sqrt{7}\\sin(\\sqrt{7}x)+B\\sqrt{7}\\cos(\\sqrt{7}x)-\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty n a_n\\sin(nx).\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative condition $y'(0)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =-A\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+B\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\ 0 & =0+B\\sqrt{7}-0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because the total solution is an even function, its derivative is an odd function, which must vanish at $x=0.$\n\nNext, we apply $y(0)=3.$ With $B=0,$ the total solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)\\sim A\\cos(\\sqrt{7}x)+\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n\\cos(nx).\n$$\n\nApplying $y(0)=3$ and noting that $\\cos(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =A+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} \\\\ A & =3-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)\\sim \n\\cos(\\sqrt{7}x)\\left(3-\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n\\right)\n+\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n\\cos(nx).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "edbe311b-6f4f-5c28-96fa-394b23f30390" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14782 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example of a Second-Order IVP"} :step/id #uuid "3b6f9bb1-ddc0-5924-bda7-a089e2b187ec" :step/math-academy-id 607587 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231815}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231815 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231419 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231293 :question/id #uuid "97290d74-457b-522d-991d-7891e5a01d28" :question/math-academy-id "e-21876"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81f5380b-7a7d-52c5-a530-3768e7cf3143" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455496}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs Using Fourier Series"} :step/id #uuid "3a843d13-ef46-5939-817d-692b8c15b4c0" :step/math-academy-id 607588}] :activity/title "Solving IVPs Using Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231521}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508156} :activity/id #uuid "07d6e91f-8b7b-5563-b227-9803067c1e75" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049479 :topic/id #uuid "f868b857-f4b2-5b40-8da2-046b9db36a57" :topic/math-academy-id 6721} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508132 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365933 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4aa6e47-9d1e-5779-9908-ea76c7a10b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365935} {:answer/id #uuid "0c1f60b5-ed12-5102-ae8c-d5d79e17c1db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-e^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507615}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507615} :answer-field/id #uuid "47512ec8-98e6-55de-bf8d-1850316def72" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4a214757-6d04-5715-a737-0db47732a8cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-343727" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=f(x),\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$  \n{{field-1}} \n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}+3y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}+3y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{-3x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=-1.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A{e}^{-3(0)}+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ -1 & =A(1)+0 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=-{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-{e}^{-3x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "57c40029-7a9d-590e-8482-18a486295420" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508135}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508134 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365960 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab86f29f-86b9-5139-b7b9-52289787dff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{36}{n{\\pi }^{2}(36-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365962} {:answer/id #uuid "13c96fa5-5aad-5a39-9717-f46276309fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(36-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365963} {:answer/id #uuid "99149897-b6f0-53e2-8cbd-173ffe25f56c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365964} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7d6966-abab-5d76-b3f7-3f492dcb3ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365964} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3e8b6be-52c3-5fb8-87bd-b14fedfa5492" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c618b807-0d3f-5c96-b840-87c2666fd829" :question/math-academy-id "q-344047" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+4{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $6$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{36(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{36(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+4{\\pi }^{2}=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm 2\\pi \\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+0+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$ First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼B(2\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(36-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\nApplying ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =B(2\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(36-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B(2\\pi ) & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(36-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B & =-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(36-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{2n{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into ${y}_{c}=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)$, we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{36(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5fca549c-1acc-5d66-bb4d-25ca00cb6c5e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508135 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91505a79-2ea5-54d7-95d8-90e5cea46420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365938} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e484f7-c12f-51da-8084-5c596183b805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507911}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507911} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa3102c8-b53d-52e0-8f47-cacae0ffde3c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0bafe4c7-5c26-53ec-8fd3-af5a2bac498d" :question/math-academy-id "q-343732" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=f(x),\\,y(0)=5\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$  \n{{field-1}} \n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}-4y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}-4y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=5.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A{e}^{4(0)}+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 5 & =A(1)+0 \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=5{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼5{e}^{4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "62326fde-cea3-5a99-a3e3-e672607ec1fa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508158}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508156 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a6e6ab0-e1a2-5036-a674-a8a7f4eb423c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{n{\\pi }^{2}(18-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365929} {:answer/id #uuid "57b6c77a-6893-516a-8f91-0dedf6e9058a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365930} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e4895c-f6d4-5497-8a22-e8d6e6d2cefc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365931} {:answer/id #uuid "7cdb14d7-b5e7-5001-bea2-034d9be5ca5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(18-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365932}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365931} :answer-field/id #uuid "e77f080d-827b-51df-b338-49ffb15b987a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "94df4605-16e7-5b93-975e-109a28027292" :question/math-academy-id "q-343447" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+2{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $6$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+2{\\pi }^{2}=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\pi \\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+0+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$ First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼B\\sqrt{2}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(18-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nApplying ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =B\\sqrt{2}\\pi +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(18-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B\\sqrt{2}\\pi  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(18-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(18-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{\\sqrt{2}{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into ${y}_{c}=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x)$, we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d35c11bd-f0d7-595a-b549-5b8b757eba5d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508132}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508158 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508036 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6cc1010-4fa1-546b-948e-840f04280c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e^{8-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507932}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507932} :answer-field/id #uuid "d219bdc7-f555-5794-9513-0ba34124b55e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "02669dc0-8a6e-5ef4-aa36-77e644af949e" :question/math-academy-id "q-343799" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=f(x),\\,y(4)=3\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $4$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(4)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}+2y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}+2y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{-2x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(4)=3.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4) & =A{e}^{-2(4)}+{y}_{p}(4) \\\\ 3 & =A{e}^{-8}+0 \\\\ A & =3{e}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=(3{e}^{8}){e}^{-2x}=3{e}^{8-2x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼3{e}^{8-2x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d7ac7bce-1bb6-5197-aa1a-34fb4ea4e07e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508134}}] :activity/title "Solving IVPs Using Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507597}]}
